WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.470
Let's go next to Luciane from England, you're
on the air.

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:06.800
Hello, David, how are you?

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.950
I'm good, how are you?

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:09.950
I'm doing well.

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:15.620
All right, so, um, so I saw that a few days
ago, you responded to a caller who was asking

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:19.689
about the idea of having conversations with
other public speakers like yourself, but on

00:00:19.689 --> 00:00:20.770
the right.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:21.810
Yes.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:28.089
And I'm sorry you spoke about it again since,
but I haven't quite caught up on this, OK?

00:00:28.089 --> 00:00:30.859
You seemed very open to the idea at the end
of that.

00:00:30.859 --> 00:00:33.910
And I think you asked the audience if they'd
be interested.

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:35.330
Right.

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:36.490
So my thoughts.

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:40.810
I've had thoughts on this for a while, wanted
to speak to you about it because I think I'm

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:44.980
quite worried that people can become quite
insular and sort of stick to their own kind.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:49.200
So to say I'm taking everything these people
say is gospel.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.600
And I wonder to what extent you think this
has contributed to the polarization we see

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:54.600
today.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:58.710
You're saying that maybe not enough conversations
between left and right are happening and that

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.790
leads to more polarization.

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:02.060
Yes.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:06.210
And if you did say and understand that you
said, it's quite hard to make it a productive

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:07.210
thing.

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:08.210
Yeah, maybe.

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:10.119
Especially if you're trying to speak to the
likes of Tucker.

00:01:10.119 --> 00:01:15.850
But would you be sort of willing to try it
and maybe leave maybe sort of like lead by

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:18.659
example to get the public to be more open
to bipartisanship?

00:01:18.659 --> 00:01:21.060
Yeah, I mean, listen, here's the thing.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.969
The call that I got last week from people
saying why not do more of the kind of like

00:01:24.969 --> 00:01:28.969
blood sport type debates with kooky right
wingers?

00:01:28.969 --> 00:01:34.140
That's very different than when, like, for
example, I had on Neil Ferguson, who's someone

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:38.789
from the right who we can engage in a more
meaningful way to hopefully try to understand

00:01:38.789 --> 00:01:39.789
each other.

00:01:39.789 --> 00:01:43.829
Or like when I had on who else, there's a
there's a bunch of different people I've had

00:01:43.829 --> 00:01:47.109
on where it's sort of more normal in a sense.

00:01:47.109 --> 00:01:48.520
I think that that's great.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.600
And I have been doing that and continue to
do that.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:55.969
I think that the question from the from the
caller was more like more cartoonish debates

00:01:55.969 --> 00:02:01.509
with Tim Pool or like the Westboro Baptist
Church lunatics or stuff like that, where

00:02:01.509 --> 00:02:03.579
that I don't see as as productive.

00:02:03.579 --> 00:02:09.700
But, you know, like when I have Neil Ferguson
on or people like that, I think that that

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:10.890
that that is good.

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:13.060
And I plan to continue doing that.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:15.760
Yes, I really hope it's making a difference.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.530
I just think both sides really love to demonize
each other so much.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:23.900
Yeah, I think if if the people we look up
to for this kind of information, if we see

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:28.290
them sit down and talk constructively, it
won't fix the problem.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:29.430
But I think it could go a long way.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:30.430
I don't disagree.

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:31.740
I'm completely on board.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:36.020
And yeah, my interpretation of last week's
question was more about like the wacky right

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:40.240
wingers, where it's just like, you know, how
much do we really it's like funny to do a

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:44.640
Jesse Lee Peterson interview, but it's kind
of a waste of time, too, of course.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:45.640
Yeah.

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:46.640
Thank you very much.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:47.640
All right, thank you so much.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:48.680
Yeah, very, very good point.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:04:11.340
Glad to have the opportunity to clarify and
explore further.

